  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1073,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1089,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153463,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> genital rhabdomyoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153464,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153465,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours arise in the vagina. Less frequently, they develop in the cervix or vulva.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153466,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital rhabdomyomas are often asymptomatic and discovered during clinical examination for other reasons {22325314}. When symptomatic, these lesions can cause dyspareunia, bleeding, or urinary tract compression.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153467,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital rhabdomyoma is the least common subtype of extracardiac rhabdomyoma. Because of the rarity of these tumours, with &lt;&nbsp;30&nbsp;reported cases in the vagina and&nbsp;3 in the cervix, epidemiological data remain limited {22325314; 28700439; 32769342}. It generally occurs in young to middle-aged female individuals (median age: 60&nbsp;years; range 30&ndash;65&nbsp;years).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153468,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In contrast to fetal rhabdomyomas, genital rhabdomyomas have not been reported in association with basal cell naevus syndrome.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153469,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>One cervical tumour revealed activation of the AKT/mTOR signalling pathway by immunohistochemical analysis {26247533}. A genome-wide microarray study did not detect somatic copy-number alterations or allelic loss events in 2&nbsp;cases of genital rhabdomyoma {28700439}. There was no evidence of somatic changes involving hedgehog pathway genes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153470,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rhabdomyoma is often a solitary polypoid or nodular lesion &lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;mm, with a grey, rubbery to glistening cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153471,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital rhabdomyomas are characterized by a subepithelial proliferation of polygonal, spindled, and/or elongated rhabdomyoblasts set in fibrous stroma. Some rhabdomyoblasts exhibit distinct cross striations. A cambium layer and mitotic activity are absent, but scattered cells with cytological atypia may be observed {28700439}. Tumour cells are positive for desmin and markers of skeletal muscle differentiation, including myogenin and MYOD1.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153472,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Smears show tissue fragments and scattered cells containing abundant eosinophilic, granular cytoplasm, peripherally placed round nuclei, and prominent nucleoli {19217050; 21053538}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153473,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153474,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> well-circumscribed subepithelial proliferation of mature rhabdomyoblasts without atypia and mitotic activity; lack of cambium layer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153475,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153476,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 226,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Genital rhabdomyoma is benign and cured by total excision {22325314; 28700439}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T14:44:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]